{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Why should the name of our father be diminished.</b> That is, according to what they said in the Mishnah in the chapter Yesh Nochalin (Bava Batra 116a), and this is their language: The daughters of Zelophehad took three portions in the inheritance — the portion of their father, and his portion with his brothers in the property of Hepher, and because he was a firstborn, etc., end quote. And this is the verse's saying <b>why should it be diminished</b> etc., corresponding to the portion of Zelophehad; <b>give us a possession among the brothers of our father</b> — meaning, the portion of Zelophehad that pertains to him among his brothers in the property of Hepher, who was among those who came out of Egypt; and the portion of the firstborn is included in the phrase ״among the brothers״ etc. This approach, however, is only according to the one who says the land was apportioned to those who came out of Egypt; but according to the one who says it was apportioned to those who came into the land, they did not take the portion of Zelophehad himself. And the verse may be explained in another way, according to what they said there (Bava Batra 118a), and this is their language: Rav Pappa said to Abaye, according to the one who says it was apportioned to those who came out of Egypt, etc., that is why it is written ״חַבְלֵי מְנַשֶּׁה עֲשָׂרָה״ — six for the six ancestral houses and four for them [the daughters]. Meaning: one, the portion of Zelophehad; two, his portion in Hepher; three, his firstborn's double portion; four, his portion in the inheritance of his brothers. But according to the one who says to those who came into the land, etc., and he resolves: he had two paternal brothers, end quote. From this it follows that even according to the opinion of the one who says it was apportioned to those who came into the land, the daughters took two inheritances — the inheritance of Zelophehad from his father as a double portion, and the inheritance of the two brothers who died. Therefore they said <b>why should it be diminished</b> etc., corresponding to the inheritance of Hepher that reverts to Zelophehad; <b>give us</b> etc., corresponding to the inheritance of the brothers who died, which also reverts to Zelophehad." "<b>Further</b>, the verse may be explained according to two interpretations by noting its saying ״<b>why should the name of our father be diminished</b>,״ when it should have said ״why should the inheritance of our father be diminished״? Further, why did they repeat saying <b>for he has no</b> son, after they had already said ״and he had no sons״? And the verse is explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory, in tractate Bava Batra (119a), and this is their language: It was taught: The daughters of Zelophehad, etc., were wise; they spoke at the appropriate moment. For Rabbi Shmuel taught that Moses was sitting and expounding the section of yibbum (levirate marriage), as it is said ״when brothers dwell together״ etc. They said to him: If we are considered like a son, give us an inheritance; and if not — let our mother be subjected to yibbum, end quote. And this is what the verse says here: <b>why should the name of our father be diminished</b> — meaning, this is the claim of yibbum, to establish the name of the deceased. Therefore they were precise in their language: ״why should the name of our father be diminished from among his family,״ and the diminution is ״for he has no son,״ and HaShem commanded ״when brothers dwell together״ etc. ״and he has no son״ etc. ״his brother's widow shall come to him״ etc. What can you say — that a daughter is considered like a son and exempts [from yibbum]? If so, ״give us a possession among the brothers of our father.״ And although Rabbi Shmuel placed the daughters' claim of inheritance before the claim of yibbum, he intended only to state the two claims they made and was not precise about stating them in order. And this approach exists both according to the opinion of Rabbi Yoshiya, who said the land was apportioned to those who came out of Egypt, and according to the opinion of Rabbi Yonatan, who said it was apportioned to those who came into the land. According to the one who says to those who came out of Egypt, they ask also for the portion of Zelophehad himself, and the remaining portions that pertain to him are understood on their own; and according to the one who says it was apportioned to those who came into the land, they ask for the portions that revert to Zelophehad."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:31.133657400Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}